  ;; process/spawn -- run ONE program and answer "<exit>\n<stdout>".
  ;;
  ;; ONE request grammar and ONE grant on every host that answers wire 20
  ;; (superproject adr-2609251400 C3, 2026-09-25; the owner's decision that
  ;; the kexe loader answers it too). Until then there were two: the kbb js
  ;; host took a decimal INDEX into the policy's invocation table (the guest
  ;; supplied no command bytes), the loader-less Linux static ELF took the
  ;; argv form below, and the kexe loader answered the identity stub -- so
  ;; one program meant three different things on three hosts.
  ;;
  ;; Why the argv form and not the index form. The index form IS narrower:
  ;; the guest cannot choose an argument, so no argument can be injected
  ;; into a granted program (`sh -c`, `git -c core.sshCommand=`, `find
  ;; -exec`). That is recorded, not dismissed. It lost for two concrete
  ;; reasons: (1) the callers that need spawn compute their argv (amu's own
  ;; nbb modules -- classpath, package_lock, test_profile -- call spawnSync
  ;; with arguments built at run time), and a table of literal rows cannot
  ;; express them, so the index form would push those callers to a second,
  ;; wider wire; (2) the argv form's authority is still the grant's: nothing
  ;; runs but a program the grant names, by its canonical absolute path, and
  ;; the SEALED spelling is what is executed. What the argv form gives up is
  ;; stated in :grant :authority below: granting a program grants every
  ;; argument vector of it, so granting an interpreter grants arbitrary code
  ;; with the process's own uid. A policy that wants fixed invocations
  ;; grants a program that takes none.
  :process/spawn
  {:source-operation process/spawn :effect :process/spawn :compiler-wire-id 20
   :kotoba.security/classification :restricted
   :source-status :friendly-qualified
   :request "<program>[ARG_SEP<arg>]..."
   :request-rules
   ["ARG_SEP separates the program from each argument; there is no quoting, and an argument cannot contain ARG_SEP"
    "<program> is non-empty; a request holding a NUL byte, or an empty program, is malformed (a plain trap, not a named refusal)"
    "<program> containing / must be byte-equal to a granted program; a bare name is looked up ONLY among the granted programs' last segments, ONLY when :path-lookup is true, and $PATH is never read"
    "the guest's spelling is argv[0]; the granted spelling is what is executed"
    "at most 32 arguments after the program, and at most 4,096 bytes of argv strings with their NULs"]
   :answer "<exit>\n<stdout>: the child's exit code in decimal, -1 when a signal ended it; then exactly the bytes it wrote to stdout"
   :child
   ["environment: empty except the variables :env names, copied from the host process's own environment"
    "stdin: a pipe at EOF; stdout: captured, at most 16,384 bytes; stderr: the host's own fd 2"
    "no descriptor but 0, 1 and 2 (the rest are closed across execve)"
    "working directory: the :fs/app-data scope root when one is granted (the first entry when a host holds several), else inherited"
    "no_new_privs where the OS has it (Linux: PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS; macOS and the js host have no equivalent -- recorded, not emulated)"
    "a 10 s wall deadline; the direct child is killed with SIGKILL when it passes (its own children are not -- there is no process group)"
    "the capability grant is not inherited: the child is an ordinary process with the host's uid, filesystem and network (NOT a sandbox)"]
   :grant
   {:shape {:programs ["<canonical absolute path>"] :path-lookup false :env ["<NAME>"]}
    :programs "1..16 distinct canonical absolute paths: no empty, . or .. segment, no trailing /, not /"
    :path-lookup "true or false (default false); when true the programs' last segments must differ"
    :env "0..16 names, each 1..255 bytes without = or NUL (default none)"
    :authority "a granted program may be run with ANY argument vector the grammar admits"
    :carriers
    {:amu-compile-policy ":spawn (top level, target-neutral); the linux-static target also reads the older :linux-static :spawn spelling, and refuses a policy that spells it both ways"
     :kexe-loader "KEXE_CAP_RESOURCES_20 (programs, colon-separated), KEXE_SPAWN_ENV (names, colon-separated), KEXE_SPAWN_PATH_LOOKUP=1; a packaged command bakes all three in and ignores the environment"
     :kbb-js ":kotoba.policy/spawn in the kbb policy, with :proc/exec (the kbb name kbb compiles to :process/spawn) in :kotoba.policy/capabilities"}}
   :refusals
   {:reason-prefix ":spawn/"
    :trap "KEXE_TRAP {:kind :capability :capability :process/spawn :reason :spawn/<name>}"
    :exit :capability/refused
    :names [:argv-budget :program-not-granted :path-lookup-denied :output-budget
            :timeout :exec-failed :isolation-unavailable :setup-failed]}
   ;; Every host that answers wire 20 takes this grammar: the static image and
   ;; the kexe loader from ONE compiled decision module (amu
   ;; tools/linux_static_handlers.kotoba; the loader runs the program from
   ;; its unsandboxed supervisor), the kbb js host (kotoba-lang/kotoba
   ;; bin/kbb_js.cljk) restating the same rules in its own provider.
   :answered-by #{:linux-static :kexe-loader :kbb-js}}
