   :compiler-wire-id 40
   :kotoba.security/classification :internal}
  ;; io/read — the bytes a COMMAND reads from its standard input. Wire id 41.
  ;;
  ;; Measured 2026-09-16 over 1,268,018 Bash calls in 558 agent transcripts
  ;; (superproject ADR-2609161710): head is invoked as a LATER pipeline
  ;; segment 97% of the time, tail 94%, cut 97%, tr 99%, sort 97%, uniq 99%,
  ;; wc 84%, awk 81%, grep 67%. A later segment reads standard input. Every
  ;; command built on :cli/args + :fs/app-data + :io/write can only be the
  ;; FIRST segment, so without this wire the commands cannot sit where they
  ;; are called.
  ;;
  ;; Two request forms. The EMPTY request answers everything up to EOF; a
  ;; DECIMAL request answers at most that many bytes of what has not been
  ;; answered yet, and the empty string at EOF -- the form `head` needs to
  ;; stop reading when it has its lines, so that `yes | head` ends. Both
  ;; forms share one cursor: bytes are answered once.
  ;;
  ;; Its own effect and NOT :host/fs-app-data: the guest names no path and
  ;; the grant carries no scope. Standard input is whatever the CALLER
  ;; connected -- a pipe, a file the caller could open, a terminal -- and a
  ;; grant to read it must not become a grant to open files, nor the other
  ;; way round. It is also NOT :host/cli-args, though both describe how the
  ;; process was invoked: argv is bounded and known at start, stdin is
  ;; unbounded and arrives as the guest asks.
  :io/read
  {:source-operation io/read :effect :host/io-read
   :compiler-wire-id 41
   :kotoba.security/classification :internal}
  ;; gpu/compute — a compute dispatch on the host's GPU. Wire id 42. Root
